# Messages only. Nothing here starts a service, enables a unit, creates a user
# or touches a config — a pacman hook is the wrong place for any of that, and
# install/arch.sh is the right one.

post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  GameCore's files are in /opt/GameCore. The box is NOT set up yet.

  To finish — emulators, Python venv, node modules, services, auto-login,
  and the kiosk session:

      sudo gamecore-setup

  It is interactive, and safe to re-run. For a scripted install:

      sudo gamecore-setup --unattended /path/to/install.conf

  (template: /opt/GameCore/install/install.conf.example)

  Nothing about your machine has changed until you run it.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  /opt/GameCore has been replaced with the new release.

  ROMs (emu/), config/ and your emulators are untouched — they do not live in
  the package. If the services are already installed, restart them:

      sudo systemctl restart gamecore-backend gamecore-ui

  Note that a box set up by `gamecore-setup` also updates itself over the air
  from the Settings screen. Driving it from BOTH pacman and OTA means two
  things write to /opt/GameCore with no idea the other exists. Pick one.

MSG
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  Removing the package deletes /opt/GameCore, but it does NOT undo what
  `gamecore-setup` did to this machine — services, auto-login, sudoers, udev
  rules and the Caddy config stay.

  Undo those FIRST, while the files are still there:

      sudo bash /opt/GameCore/install/uninstall.sh --dry-run   # look
      sudo bash /opt/GameCore/install/uninstall.sh             # do it

  The uninstaller reads the manifest in /var/lib/gamecore/, so it only reverts
  what this box's install actually changed.

MSG
}
